[
{ch:22,q:"What is the essential feature of a nucleophile?",choices:["It has a lone pair of electrons it can donate","It has a positive charge","It has a pi bond it can donate","It is always negatively charged"],explain:"A nucleophile forms a bond to its reaction partner by donating both bonding electrons; nucleophiles are electron rich, typically having one or more lone pairs, and may or may not carry a negative charge."},
{ch:22,q:"Which of the following species can act as a nucleophile: NH<sub>3</sub>, CH<sub>4</sub>, H<sub>2</sub>O?",choices:["NH<sub>3</sub> and H<sub>2</sub>O only","CH<sub>4</sub> and H<sub>2</sub>O only","NH<sub>3</sub> and CH<sub>4</sub> only","NH<sub>3</sub>, CH<sub>4</sub> and H<sub>2</sub>O"],explain:"NH<sub>3</sub> and H<sub>2</sub>O both have lone pairs available to donate, so they can act as nucleophiles. CH<sub>4</sub> has no lone pairs and cannot act as a nucleophile."},
{ch:22,q:"Why is a nucleophile described as 'nucleus-loving'?",choices:["Being electron rich, it is attracted to positive charges such as an atomic nucleus or a region of partial positive charge","It always bonds directly to the nucleus of another atom, bypassing the electron shells","It repels all negatively charged species","It only reacts with neutral, non-polar molecules"],explain:"The name reflects the nucleophile's electron-rich character: it is attracted towards positive charges, such as a nucleus or a &delta;+ region of a molecule, hence 'nucleus-loving'."},
{ch:22,q:"In the reaction R&ndash;X + OH<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; R&ndash;OH + X<sup>&minus;</sup>, what role does X<sup>&minus;</sup> play?",choices:["Leaving group","Nucleophile","Electrophile","Reducing agent"],explain:"X<sup>&minus;</sup> departs from the substrate as the C&ndash;X bond breaks; it is the leaving group. OH<sup>&minus;</sup> is the nucleophile in this reaction."},
{ch:22,q:"Why are halogenoalkanes susceptible to attack by nucleophiles at the carbon atom bonded to the halogen?",choices:["The halogen is more electronegative than carbon, making that carbon atom electron deficient (&delta;+)","The halogen is less electronegative than carbon, making that carbon atom electron rich","Halogen atoms always carry a full negative charge in halogenoalkanes","Carbon atoms are always electron deficient regardless of what they are bonded to"],explain:"The halogen is more electronegative than carbon and pulls the shared electron density towards itself, leaving the halogen &delta;&minus; and the attached carbon &delta;+, making it attractive to nucleophiles."},
{ch:22,q:"Why do halogens generally make good leaving groups in nucleophilic substitution reactions?",choices:["They form relatively weak bonds with carbon and are highly electronegative","They form very strong bonds with carbon that resist breaking","They are always positively charged when attached to carbon","They cannot be replaced by any other nucleophile"],explain:"Halogens make good leaving groups because they form relatively weak bonds with carbon, and their high electronegativity draws electron density away from carbon, favouring nucleophilic attack there."},
{ch:22,q:"In the mechanism for R&ndash;X + Nu:<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; R&ndash;Nu + X<sup>&minus;</sup>, which bond forms and which bond breaks?",choices:["A new C&ndash;Nu bond forms while the C&ndash;X bond breaks, both involving the same carbon atom","A new C&ndash;Nu bond forms while an X<sup>&minus;</sup>&ndash;X bond breaks","The C&ndash;X bond forms while a C&ndash;Nu bond breaks","Two new bonds form on different carbon atoms"],explain:"The nucleophile donates an electron pair to form a new bond to the carbon originally bonded to the leaving group, while the C&ndash;X bond on that same carbon breaks heterolytically, releasing X<sup>&minus;</sup>."},
{ch:22,q:"When a neutral nucleophile such as water reacts with a halogenoalkane, what is the immediate charge on the substrate-derived intermediate before deprotonation?",choices:["Positive","Negative","Neutral","It depends only on the identity of the halogen"],explain:"Because the neutral water molecule donates a lone pair without losing its own atoms, the oxygen forms a third bond and the intermediate R&ndash;OH<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup> carries a positive charge, before losing H<sup>+</sup> to give the neutral alcohol product."},
{ch:22,q:"What is heterolytic fission?",choices:["The breaking of a covalent bond so that both bonding electrons end up on one of the two fragments","The breaking of a covalent bond so that each fragment gets one bonding electron","The formation of a new covalent bond from two lone pairs","The breaking of an ionic bond in aqueous solution"],explain:"Heterolytic fission produces two oppositely charged ions because both bonding electrons remain with one fragment; this contrasts with homolytic fission (Reactivity 3.3), which splits the electron pair equally to form two radicals."},
{ch:22,q:"Which equation correctly represents the heterolytic fission of hydrogen chloride?",choices:["H&ndash;Cl &rarr; H<sup>+</sup> + Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>","H&ndash;Cl &rarr; H<sup>&bull;</sup> + Cl<sup>&bull;</sup>","H&ndash;Cl &rarr; H<sup>&minus;</sup> + Cl<sup>+</sup>","2H&ndash;Cl &rarr; H<sub>2</sub> + Cl<sub>2</sub>"],explain:"In heterolytic fission both bonding electrons go to the more electronegative chlorine atom, giving H<sup>+</sup> and Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>."},
{ch:22,q:"What type of curved arrow is used to represent the movement of an electron pair, as in heterolytic fission?",choices:["A double-barbed arrow with a full arrowhead","A single-barbed arrow, known as a fish hook","A straight dashed line with no arrowhead","A double-headed equilibrium arrow"],explain:"A full, double-barbed curly arrow represents the movement of a whole electron pair, used for heterolytic fission and for nucleophile/electrophile reactions; a single-barbed 'fish hook' arrow is instead used for the single electrons of homolytic (radical) processes."},
{ch:22,q:"Why does the more electronegative atom in a bond keep both electrons during heterolytic fission?",choices:["It attracts the shared electron density more strongly, so it is favoured to retain both electrons and become the negative ion","Electronegativity has no bearing on which atom keeps the electrons","The less electronegative atom always keeps both electrons instead","Both atoms always keep exactly one electron each, regardless of electronegativity"],explain:"The more electronegative atom pulls the shared electron pair towards itself; in heterolytic fission it ends up keeping both bonding electrons and becomes the negative ion, while the less electronegative atom becomes the positive ion."},
{ch:22,q:"What is the essential feature of an electrophile?",choices:["It can accept a pair of electrons from another species","It has a lone pair of electrons it can donate","It always carries a full negative charge","It always contains a halogen atom"],explain:"An electrophile forms a bond to its reaction partner (the nucleophile) by accepting both bonding electrons from that partner; electrophiles are typically electron deficient or carry a positive/partial positive charge."},
{ch:22,q:"Which of the following species can act as an electrophile: HBr, Br<sub>2</sub>, NH<sub>3</sub>?",choices:["HBr and Br<sub>2</sub> only","NH<sub>3</sub> and Br<sub>2</sub> only","HBr and NH<sub>3</sub> only","HBr, Br<sub>2</sub> and NH<sub>3</sub>"],explain:"HBr and Br<sub>2</sub> can both act as electrophiles (Br<sub>2</sub> becomes polarized on approach to an electron-rich double bond). NH<sub>3</sub> has a lone pair and acts as a nucleophile, not an electrophile."},
{ch:22,q:"According to the nature of science discussion, why is describing electrophiles simply as 'electron deficient' too narrow a definition?",choices:["It would wrongly exclude molecules such as Br<sub>2</sub>, which are not electron deficient but still act as electrophiles","It would wrongly include all nucleophiles as electrophiles too","Electrophiles are actually always electron rich, not electron deficient","It excludes cations, which cannot act as electrophiles"],explain:"Bromine, Br<sub>2</sub>, is not itself electron deficient, yet it acts as an electrophile in addition reactions with alkenes as it becomes polarized on approach, so a definition restricted to 'electron deficient' species would be too narrow."},
{ch:22,q:"Why are alkenes more reactive than alkanes towards electrophiles?",choices:["The carbon-carbon double bond has a high electron density that attracts electrophiles","Alkenes contain more hydrogen atoms per carbon than alkanes","Alkenes are always polar molecules, unlike alkanes","Alkanes have more electron-dense bonds than alkenes"],explain:"The C=C double bond has a high electron density, which makes it attractive to electrophiles and allows alkenes to undergo addition reactions that alkanes cannot."},
{ch:22,q:"What type of reaction occurs when an electrophile adds across the carbon-carbon double bond of an alkene?",choices:["Electrophilic addition","Nucleophilic substitution","Electrophilic substitution","Radical substitution"],explain:"Alkenes undergo electrophilic addition reactions, in which the double bond breaks and an electrophile adds across the two carbon atoms to give a saturated product."},
{ch:22,q:"What is the product of the reaction between ethene and water, using a concentrated sulfuric acid catalyst?",choices:["Ethanol","Ethane","Ethanoic acid","Bromoethane"],explain:"CH<sub>2</sub>=CH<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH (ethanol), a hydration reaction that requires a strong acid catalyst since water is a poor electrophile on its own."},
{ch:22,q:"What is the product when propene reacts with bromine, Br<sub>2</sub>?",choices:["1,2-dibromopropane","1-bromopropane","1,1-dibromopropane","Propene does not react with bromine"],explain:"Br<sub>2</sub> adds across the C=C double bond of propene, with one bromine atom bonding to each of the two former double-bond carbons, giving 1,2-dibromopropane."},
{ch:22,q:"What visible change occurs when an alkene reacts with bromine water in the dark?",choices:["The bromine water is decolorized rapidly, without any need for UV light","The bromine water turns a deeper orange colour","No visible change occurs unless UV light is present","The solution turns blue"],explain:"Alkenes react with Br<sub>2</sub> via electrophilic addition, which does not require UV light, so bromine water is decolorized rapidly even in the dark. Alkanes only react via a radical mechanism that requires UV light to initiate."},
{ch:22,q:"What is the reactivity order of the hydrogen halides in their addition reactions with alkenes?",choices:["HI &gt; HBr &gt; HCl","HCl &gt; HBr &gt; HI","HBr &gt; HI &gt; HCl","All three hydrogen halides react at the same rate"],explain:"Reactivity is HI &gt; HBr &gt; HCl because the strength of the hydrogen-halogen bond decreases down group 17, so the weakest bond (H&ndash;I) breaks most easily."},
{ch:22,q:"What is the product when ethene reacts with hydrogen chloride, HCl?",choices:["Chloroethane","1,2-dichloroethane","Ethane","Chloroethene"],explain:"CH<sub>2</sub>=CH<sub>2</sub> + HCl &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>Cl (chloroethane); HCl adds across the double bond, with H bonding to one carbon and Cl to the other."},
{ch:22,q:"1-Iodopropane, C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>7</sub>I, reacts with aqueous sodium hydroxide via nucleophilic substitution. Which species acts as the nucleophile in this reaction?",choices:["Hydroxide ion, OH<sup>&minus;</sup>","Iodide ion, I<sup>&minus;</sup>","1-iodopropane, C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>7</sub>I","Sodium ion, Na<sup>+</sup>"],explain:"The hydroxide ion, OH<sup>&minus;</sup>, has a lone pair it donates to the electron-deficient carbon, acting as the nucleophile; the iodide ion, I<sup>&minus;</sup>, is the leaving group that departs from the substrate."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: What distinguishes a primary halogenoalkane from a tertiary one?",choices:["A primary halogenoalkane has the C&ndash;X carbon bonded to at least two hydrogen atoms; a tertiary one has that carbon bonded to three alkyl groups and no hydrogens","A primary halogenoalkane always has more carbon atoms than a tertiary one","A tertiary halogenoalkane always reacts faster via S<sub>N</sub>2 than a primary one","There is no structural difference; the terms refer only to reaction rate"],explain:"A primary halogenoalkane has the carbon bonded to the halogen attached to at least two hydrogens (and at most one alkyl group); a tertiary halogenoalkane has that carbon bonded to three alkyl groups, with no hydrogens on it."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Which mechanism is favoured by primary halogenoalkanes reacting with a nucleophile?",choices:["S<sub>N</sub>2","S<sub>N</sub>1","Electrophilic addition","Electrophilic substitution"],explain:"Primary halogenoalkanes, with little steric hindrance around the C&ndash;X carbon, favour the S<sub>N</sub>2 mechanism: a single concerted step with direct backside attack by the nucleophile."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: What does the '2' in S<sub>N</sub>2 refer to?",choices:["The reaction is bimolecular/second order overall, since the rate depends on both the halogenoalkane and the nucleophile","Exactly two halogenoalkane molecules react together in every event","Two separate steps make up the mechanism","Two different nucleophiles must be present simultaneously"],explain:"S<sub>N</sub>2 stands for substitution, nucleophilic, bimolecular (second order): the rate equation is rate = k[halogenoalkane][nucleophile], since both species appear in the single-step transition state."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Which rate equation correctly describes an S<sub>N</sub>2 reaction?",choices:["rate = k[halogenoalkane][nucleophile]","rate = k[halogenoalkane]","rate = k[nucleophile]","rate = k[halogenoalkane]<sup>2</sup>"],explain:"Because the S<sub>N</sub>2 mechanism is a single step involving both the substrate and the nucleophile simultaneously, the rate depends on the concentration of both: rate = k[halogenoalkane][nucleophile], second order overall."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: In the S<sub>N</sub>2 mechanism, from which direction does the nucleophile attack the substrate carbon?",choices:["Directly opposite the leaving group (a 'backside attack')","From the same side as the leaving group","From directly above the plane of the carbon only","The direction is random and has no effect on the product"],explain:"In S<sub>N</sub>2, the nucleophile attacks from 180&deg; opposite the leaving group, a 'backside attack', which is what causes inversion of configuration at that carbon."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Why is the S<sub>N</sub>2 mechanism described as 'concerted'?",choices:["Bond formation (C&ndash;Nu) and bond breaking (C&ndash;X) occur simultaneously, in a single step with no intermediate","It involves two completely separate and independent steps","It requires the presence of two different catalysts at once","It can only occur at exactly 2 degrees Celsius"],explain:"A concerted mechanism is one in which bond breaking and bond forming happen together in a single transition state, with no intermediate species formed in between, as in S<sub>N</sub>2."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: What happens to the three-dimensional arrangement of the other groups on the carbon during an S<sub>N</sub>2 reaction?",choices:["It is inverted, like an umbrella turning inside out in the wind","It remains completely unchanged","It becomes planar and symmetric","It is inverted only if the nucleophile is negatively charged"],explain:"Because the nucleophile attacks from the side opposite the leaving group, the other three groups are pushed through the plane of the carbon to the opposite side as the reaction proceeds, inverting the spatial arrangement."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Why is the S<sub>N</sub>2 mechanism described as stereospecific?",choices:["Because bond formation and bond breaking are linked in one transition state, the stereochemistry of the reactant completely determines the stereochemistry of the product","Because the product is always a racemic mixture regardless of the reactant","Because no stereochemical outcome can ever be predicted for this mechanism","Because it only ever occurs with achiral substrates"],explain:"S<sub>N</sub>2 is stereospecific because the single, concerted transition state links the configuration of the reactant directly to the configuration of the product (with 100% inversion), unlike a mechanism that passes through a planar intermediate."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: If a single, optically pure enantiomer of a chiral halogenoalkane reacts entirely via S<sub>N</sub>2, what is the expected optical outcome in the product?",choices:["A single enantiomer of product, with 100% inversion of configuration, which is optically active","An exact 50:50 racemic mixture, which is optically inactive","A mixture of many different diastereomers","No optical activity can ever be measured for any product of a substitution reaction"],explain:"Because S<sub>N</sub>2 is stereospecific with a single mechanism giving complete inversion, a single pure enantiomer reacting entirely via S<sub>N</sub>2 gives a single, fully inverted enantiomer of product, retaining full optical activity."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Which mechanism is favoured by tertiary halogenoalkanes reacting with a nucleophile?",choices:["S<sub>N</sub>1","S<sub>N</sub>2","Electrophilic addition","Radical substitution"],explain:"Tertiary halogenoalkanes, with significant steric hindrance from three bulky alkyl groups, favour the S<sub>N</sub>1 mechanism, which does not require direct backside attack on the crowded carbon."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Why does steric hindrance disfavour the S<sub>N</sub>2 mechanism for tertiary halogenoalkanes?",choices:["The three bulky alkyl groups physically block a nucleophile from approaching the carbon from the side opposite the leaving group","Steric hindrance has no effect on any substitution mechanism","Tertiary halogenoalkanes have no leaving group at all","Bulky alkyl groups make the carbon more electronegative, repelling all nucleophiles"],explain:"The bulky alkyl groups around a tertiary carbon physically crowd the site that an S<sub>N</sub>2 nucleophile would need to approach from, making backside attack very difficult and so disfavouring S<sub>N</sub>2."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: What is the first step of the S<sub>N</sub>1 mechanism?",choices:["Heterolytic fission of the C&ndash;X bond, with the halide ion leaving before any nucleophile attacks, forming a carbocation intermediate","The nucleophile attacking the carbon simultaneously as the C&ndash;X bond breaks","Two separate halogenoalkane molecules combining","Homolytic fission of the C&ndash;X bond, forming two radicals"],explain:"In S<sub>N</sub>1, the first (slow) step is the halogenoalkane breaking apart on its own: the C&ndash;X bond undergoes heterolytic fission, the halide ion leaves completely, and a carbocation intermediate is formed, with no nucleophile involved yet."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: What is a carbocation?",choices:["A positively charged ion with the charge centred on a carbon atom","A negatively charged ion centred on a carbon atom","A neutral radical with an unpaired electron on carbon","An ion in which the charge is centred on an oxygen atom"],explain:"A carbocation is a cation with a positive charge localized on a carbon atom, typically formed when a carbon loses a bonding pair of electrons, as in the first step of S<sub>N</sub>1 or in electrophilic addition."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: What is the second step of the S<sub>N</sub>1 mechanism?",choices:["A nucleophile rapidly attacks the carbocation intermediate, forming the new C&ndash;Nu bond","The carbocation intermediate spontaneously decomposes with no further reaction","Another molecule of the halogenoalkane attacks the carbocation","The leaving group reattaches to reform the original halogenoalkane and the mechanism stops"],explain:"In the fast second step of S<sub>N</sub>1, a nucleophile (such as OH<sup>&minus;</sup>) attacks the positively charged carbocation intermediate, forming the new bond and completing the substitution."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: What does the '1' in S<sub>N</sub>1 refer to?",choices:["The reaction is unimolecular/first order overall, since the rate-determining step depends only on the concentration of the halogenoalkane","Exactly one nucleophile must be present for the reaction to occur at all","Only one step makes up the entire mechanism","Only one type of halogenoalkane can ever react by this mechanism"],explain:"S<sub>N</sub>1 stands for substitution, nucleophilic, unimolecular (first order): the slow, rate-determining first step involves only the halogenoalkane breaking apart, giving rate = k[halogenoalkane]."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Which rate equation correctly describes an S<sub>N</sub>1 reaction?",choices:["rate = k[halogenoalkane]","rate = k[halogenoalkane][nucleophile]","rate = k[nucleophile]","rate = k[halogenoalkane][nucleophile]<sup>2</sup>"],explain:"Because the slow, rate-determining step of S<sub>N</sub>1 involves only the halogenoalkane breaking apart (with no nucleophile involved in that step), the rate depends only on [halogenoalkane]: rate = k[halogenoalkane], first order overall."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Why does a tertiary carbocation form more readily than a primary carbocation?",choices:["The three alkyl groups around a tertiary carbocation each donate electron density via a positive inductive effect, stabilizing the positive charge more than a single alkyl group can","Alkyl groups withdraw electron density from a carbocation, destabilizing it further the more there are","Tertiary carbocations are always negatively charged, unlike primary carbocations","Carbocation stability is completely unrelated to the number of attached alkyl groups"],explain:"Each alkyl group has an electron-donating (positive) inductive effect that pushes electron density towards the positive carbon, spreading out and stabilizing the charge; more alkyl groups (as in a tertiary carbocation) give greater stabilization."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Which correctly ranks carbocation stability from most to least stable?",choices:["Tertiary &gt; secondary &gt; primary","Primary &gt; secondary &gt; tertiary","Secondary &gt; tertiary &gt; primary","All carbocations have identical stability regardless of substitution"],explain:"Carbocation stability increases with the number of adjacent alkyl groups providing a positive inductive effect, giving the order tertiary &gt; secondary &gt; primary."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Why does the greater stability of a tertiary carbocation favour the S<sub>N</sub>1 mechanism for tertiary halogenoalkanes?",choices:["A more stable carbocation persists long enough for the second step (nucleophilic attack) to occur before the intermediate decomposes or reverts","A more stable carbocation reacts with a nucleophile more slowly than an unstable one","Carbocation stability has no bearing on whether S<sub>N</sub>1 is favoured","Only unstable carbocations can ever be attacked by a nucleophile"],explain:"Because the carbocation is an intermediate that must survive long enough to be attacked by a nucleophile in the second step, a more stable (longer-lived) carbocation makes the two-step S<sub>N</sub>1 pathway viable, which is why tertiary substrates favour S<sub>N</sub>1."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Why is the S<sub>N</sub>1 mechanism described as not stereospecific?",choices:["The planar carbocation intermediate can be attacked by the nucleophile from either face with roughly equal probability, losing the original stereochemical information","Every S<sub>N</sub>1 reaction always gives 100% inversion of configuration","S<sub>N</sub>1 reactions never involve any chiral centres under any circumstances","The nucleophile can never attack a carbocation intermediate at all"],explain:"Because the sp<sup>2</sup>, planar carbocation intermediate is symmetric about its plane, a nucleophile can attack from either face with nearly equal likelihood, so the original stereochemistry of the substrate is not reliably transferred to the product."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: If a single, optically pure enantiomer of a chiral halogenoalkane reacts entirely via S<sub>N</sub>1, what is the expected optical outcome in the product?",choices:["A near 50:50 mixture of both enantiomers (a racemic mixture), which is close to optically inactive overall","A single, fully inverted enantiomer, which is optically active","A single, fully retained enantiomer identical to the starting material","An achiral product with no stereocentres at all"],explain:"Because the planar carbocation intermediate can be attacked from either face with roughly equal probability, the product is close to a racemic (50:50) mixture of both enantiomers, which has little or no net optical activity."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Why is a true 50:50 racemic mixture not always obtained in a real S<sub>N</sub>1 reaction?",choices:["The departing leaving group (or solvent) can partially shield one face of the carbocation, slightly favouring nucleophilic attack from the other face","S<sub>N</sub>1 reactions are, in reality, always perfectly stereospecific","The carbocation intermediate is never actually planar in practice","Nucleophiles can never attack a carbocation from more than one direction"],explain:"In practice, the leaving group or surrounding solvent molecules can partially shield the face of the carbocation it departed from, giving some steric preference for attack from the opposite face, so the product is often close to but not exactly racemic."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Why can the mechanism of nucleophilic substitution in secondary halogenoalkanes not be predicted with certainty?",choices:["Secondary substrates typically react through a mixture of both S<sub>N</sub>1 and S<sub>N</sub>2 pathways simultaneously, since they are intermediate in both steric hindrance and carbocation stability","Secondary halogenoalkanes never undergo nucleophilic substitution by either mechanism","Secondary halogenoalkanes always react exclusively by S<sub>N</sub>2 and never by S<sub>N</sub>1","Secondary halogenoalkanes are chemically identical to tertiary halogenoalkanes"],explain:"A secondary carbon is sterically and electronically intermediate between the open, poorly-stabilized primary case and the hindered, well-stabilized tertiary case, so experimental data shows secondary halogenoalkanes typically react via a mixture of both S<sub>N</sub>1 and S<sub>N</sub>2."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: For halogenoalkanes with the same carbon skeleton, what is the observed order of reactivity towards nucleophilic substitution?",choices:["Iodoalkanes &gt; bromoalkanes &gt; chloroalkanes &gt; fluoroalkanes","Fluoroalkanes &gt; chloroalkanes &gt; bromoalkanes &gt; iodoalkanes","Chloroalkanes &gt; bromoalkanes &gt; iodoalkanes &gt; fluoroalkanes","All four react at identical rates regardless of the halogen"],explain:"The observed reactivity order, iodoalkanes &gt; bromoalkanes &gt; chloroalkanes &gt; fluoroalkanes, follows directly from the decreasing C&ndash;X bond enthalpy down the series, since weaker bonds break more easily."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Using the bond enthalpies C&ndash;F 492, C&ndash;Cl 324, C&ndash;Br 285 and C&ndash;I 228 (all kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>), which halide ion is the best leaving group?",choices:["I<sup>&minus;</sup>, since the C&ndash;I bond is weakest and breaks most easily","F<sup>&minus;</sup>, since the C&ndash;F bond is strongest","Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>, since it is intermediate in bond strength","All four halide ions are equally good leaving groups"],explain:"The weaker the C&ndash;X bond, the more easily it breaks, and the better the corresponding halide ion is as a leaving group; since C&ndash;I has the lowest bond enthalpy (228 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>), I<sup>&minus;</sup> is the best leaving group of the four."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Does the identity of the leaving group (F, Cl, Br, I) affect the rate of both S<sub>N</sub>1 and S<sub>N</sub>2 reactions?",choices:["Yes, it affects the rate of both mechanisms, since both require the C&ndash;X bond to break","No, it only affects S<sub>N</sub>1, since only S<sub>N</sub>1 involves bond breaking","No, it only affects S<sub>N</sub>2, since only S<sub>N</sub>2 involves a leaving group","The leaving group identity has no effect on reaction rate in any mechanism"],explain:"Because the C&ndash;X bond must break in both the single step of S<sub>N</sub>2 and the first step of S<sub>N</sub>1, the strength of this bond (and hence the leaving group's identity) affects the rate of both mechanisms, independent of which one is operating."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Which type of substrate favours S<sub>N</sub>2 due to low steric hindrance?",choices:["Primary halogenoalkanes","Tertiary halogenoalkanes","Halogenoalkanes with no hydrogen atoms at all","Halogenoalkanes dissolved in a very viscous solvent"],explain:"Primary halogenoalkanes have small hydrogen atoms (rather than bulky alkyl groups) around the C&ndash;X carbon, leaving it open to backside attack by a nucleophile and so favouring S<sub>N</sub>2."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: How does nucleophile strength and concentration affect which mechanism predominates?",choices:["A strong, highly concentrated nucleophile favours S<sub>N</sub>2, since the nucleophile appears in the S<sub>N</sub>2 rate equation but not the S<sub>N</sub>1 rate equation","A strong nucleophile always favours S<sub>N</sub>1 over S<sub>N</sub>2","Nucleophile concentration has no effect on either mechanism","A weak nucleophile always reacts faster than a strong one by any mechanism"],explain:"Because rate = k[substrate][nucleophile] for S<sub>N</sub>2, increasing nucleophile strength or concentration speeds up S<sub>N</sub>2 specifically; S<sub>N</sub>1's rate (rate = k[substrate]) is unaffected by nucleophile concentration, so a weak, dilute nucleophile does not disfavour S<sub>N</sub>1 the way it disfavours S<sub>N</sub>2."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: How does a polar protic solvent (such as water or an alcohol) influence the choice between S<sub>N</sub>1 and S<sub>N</sub>2?",choices:["It stabilizes the carbocation intermediate and the leaving anion, favouring S<sub>N</sub>1, while also solvating and partially shielding the nucleophile, disfavouring S<sub>N</sub>2","It has no effect on either mechanism","It stabilizes the S<sub>N</sub>2 transition state exclusively, with no effect on S<sub>N</sub>1","It only affects reactions of tertiary halogenoalkanes"],explain:"A polar protic solvent surrounds and stabilizes the ions formed in the slow S<sub>N</sub>1 first step (favouring S<sub>N</sub>1), while also solvating and reducing the reactivity of the nucleophile needed for direct S<sub>N</sub>2 attack (disfavouring S<sub>N</sub>2)."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Which solvent condition tends to favour the S<sub>N</sub>2 mechanism?",choices:["A less polar solvent, which leaves the nucleophile less solvated and more reactive","A highly polar protic solvent that strongly stabilizes carbocations","Any solvent, since solvent polarity never affects S<sub>N</sub>2 specifically","Only the complete absence of any solvent"],explain:"A less polar (or polar aprotic) solvent does not solvate the nucleophile as strongly, leaving it more available and reactive for a direct S<sub>N</sub>2 attack, in contrast to a polar protic solvent which favours S<sub>N</sub>1 instead."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Predict the preferred mechanism for the reaction of 2-bromo-2-methylpropane with hydroxide ions, and justify this.",choices:["S<sub>N</sub>1, since this tertiary substrate suffers steric hindrance to backside attack and forms a stabilized tertiary carbocation","S<sub>N</sub>2, since tertiary substrates always react fastest by backside attack","Neither mechanism applies to tertiary halogenoalkanes","Electrophilic addition, since the molecule contains a halogen"],explain:"2-Bromo-2-methylpropane is tertiary: its three methyl groups create steric hindrance that blocks S<sub>N</sub>2 backside attack, while also stabilizing the resulting tertiary carbocation, so S<sub>N</sub>1 is strongly favoured."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Predict the preferred mechanism for the reaction of bromoethane with hydroxide ions, and justify this.",choices:["S<sub>N</sub>2, since this primary substrate has an open, unhindered carbon and would form only a poorly stabilized primary carbocation","S<sub>N</sub>1, since primary substrates always form the most stable carbocations","Neither mechanism applies to primary halogenoalkanes","Electrophilic substitution, since bromoethane contains a halogen atom"],explain:"Bromoethane is primary: its C&ndash;Br carbon is open to backside attack (favouring S<sub>N</sub>2), and any carbocation that might form would be a poorly stabilized primary carbocation (disfavouring S<sub>N</sub>1), so S<sub>N</sub>2 is strongly favoured."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Why does the presence of a chiral centre make the comparison of S<sub>N</sub>1 and S<sub>N</sub>2 experimentally testable?",choices:["The stereochemical outcome (full inversion versus racemization) differs sharply between the two mechanisms and can be measured using a polarimeter","Chiral centres make it impossible to determine which mechanism is operating","Only achiral substrates can react by either S<sub>N</sub>1 or S<sub>N</sub>2","Optical activity is unrelated to reaction mechanism in any way"],explain:"Because S<sub>N</sub>2 gives 100% inversion (retaining optical activity, inverted) while S<sub>N</sub>1 gives (approximate) racemization (losing optical activity), measuring the optical rotation of the product with a polarimeter provides direct evidence for which mechanism (or what mixture of the two) is occurring."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: A chiral halogenoalkane reacts with hydroxide ions, and the product solution shows full optical activity, fully inverted relative to the starting material. What does this suggest about the mechanism?",choices:["The reaction proceeded essentially entirely by S<sub>N</sub>2","The reaction proceeded essentially entirely by S<sub>N</sub>1","The reaction did not proceed via nucleophilic substitution at all","No conclusion about mechanism can be drawn from optical activity"],explain:"Full retention of optical activity with complete inversion is the signature of a stereospecific S<sub>N</sub>2 mechanism, since bond formation and breaking are linked in a single transition state."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: A chiral halogenoalkane reacts with hydroxide ions, and the product solution shows no net optical activity. What does this suggest about the mechanism?",choices:["The reaction proceeded essentially entirely by S<sub>N</sub>1, giving a racemic (or near-racemic) product","The reaction proceeded essentially entirely by S<sub>N</sub>2","The reaction produced only achiral products","The starting material must not have been chiral"],explain:"No net optical activity in the product indicates an essentially racemic mixture, consistent with an S<sub>N</sub>1 mechanism in which the planar carbocation intermediate is attacked from either face with roughly equal probability."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Which of the following best summarizes the difference in reaction profile between S<sub>N</sub>1 and S<sub>N</sub>2?",choices:["S<sub>N</sub>2 has one transition state and no intermediate; S<sub>N</sub>1 has two transition states with a carbocation intermediate shown as a local energy minimum between them","S<sub>N</sub>1 has one transition state and no intermediate; S<sub>N</sub>2 has two transition states with an intermediate","Both mechanisms have identical reaction profiles with a single transition state","Neither mechanism has any activation energy at all"],explain:"Because S<sub>N</sub>2 is a single concerted step, its profile shows one peak (transition state) between reactants and products; S<sub>N</sub>1's two separate steps give two peaks (transition states) separated by a local energy minimum corresponding to the carbocation intermediate."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: In the electrophilic addition mechanism, what role does the &pi; bond of an alkene play in the first step?",choices:["It behaves, in effect, as a nucleophile, attacking the electrophile with its electron pair","It behaves as an electrophile, accepting electrons from the incoming species","It remains completely unreactive throughout the mechanism","It is converted directly into a new &sigma; bond without any electron movement"],explain:"The electron-rich &pi; bond of the alkene attacks the electrophile, donating its electron pair to form a new &sigma; bond; in this sense the &pi; bond itself behaves like a nucleophile in the first step of electrophilic addition."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Why does the &pi; bond of an alkene break more easily than a &sigma; bond?",choices:["The electrons of the &pi; bond are less tightly held (further from the bonding axis) than those of a &sigma; bond, making the &pi; bond weaker","&pi; bonds are always stronger than &sigma; bonds and so react less readily","&pi; bonds contain no electrons at all","&sigma; bonds cannot exist in alkenes"],explain:"The &pi; bond's electron density lies above and below the plane of the sigma-bonded framework, less closely associated with the bonding nuclei than a &sigma; bond's electrons, making it a weaker bond that breaks preferentially during addition reactions."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: What is formed as the intermediate in the electrophilic addition mechanism, after the first step?",choices:["A carbocation intermediate","A carbanion intermediate","A stable, fully saturated final product","A new alkene with a different double bond position"],explain:"In the first (slow) step of electrophilic addition, the &pi; bond attacks the electrophile, forming one new &sigma; bond and leaving a positive charge on the other former double-bond carbon: a carbocation intermediate."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: What happens in the second step of the electrophilic addition mechanism?",choices:["A nucleophile (often the anion released in step 1) attacks the carbocation intermediate, forming the second new &sigma; bond","The carbocation intermediate simply decomposes back to the original alkene","Another molecule of alkene attacks the carbocation","The carbocation is converted directly into a radical"],explain:"In the fast second step, a nucleophile &mdash; frequently the anion (e.g. Br<sup>&minus;</sup>) released during step 1, or a solvent molecule &mdash; attacks the positively charged carbocation, completing the addition and forming the saturated product."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Why is the first step of electrophilic addition the slow, rate-determining step, while the second step is fast?",choices:["The first step involves breaking a bond and forming a less stable, higher-energy carbocation, while the second step simply involves an existing lone pair attacking an existing positive centre","The first step has no activation energy at all, making it instantaneous","The second step always involves breaking more bonds than the first step","Both steps actually occur at identical rates in every addition reaction"],explain:"Generating the reactive, relatively unstable carbocation intermediate in step 1 requires overcoming a higher activation energy, making it slow; the carbocation is then attacked rapidly by a nucleophile in step 2, which has a much lower activation energy."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Describe what happens in step 1 of the mechanism for the addition of bromine to ethene.",choices:["The non-polar Br<sub>2</sub> molecule becomes polarized as it approaches the electron-rich double bond, and the &pi; electrons attack the &delta;+ bromine, forming a C&ndash;Br bond and a carbocation while Br<sup>&minus;</sup> is released","Br<sub>2</sub> instantly adds both bromine atoms to the same carbon in a single step","The Br&ndash;Br bond undergoes homolytic fission to form two bromine radicals","Ethene is oxidized to carbon dioxide and water by bromine"],explain:"As Br<sub>2</sub> approaches the alkene, electron repulsion from the &pi; bond polarizes it (&delta;+ on the nearer Br); the &pi; electrons then attack this &delta;+ bromine, forming a new C&ndash;Br &sigma; bond, breaking the Br&ndash;Br bond heterolytically, and leaving a carbocation on the other carbon."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: In the addition of bromine to ethene, what happens in step 2?",choices:["The bromide ion released in step 1 acts as a nucleophile, attacking the carbocation to form the second C&ndash;Br bond and give 1,2-dibromoethane","A second molecule of Br<sub>2</sub> reacts with the carbocation","The carbocation reacts with water instead of bromide","No further reaction occurs after step 1"],explain:"In the fast second step, the Br<sup>&minus;</sup> ion generated in step 1 attacks the positively charged carbon of the carbocation intermediate, forming the second C&ndash;Br bond and completing the formation of 1,2-dibromoethane."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: What experimental evidence supports the existence of a genuine carbocation intermediate in the bromination of ethene?",choices:["When chloride ions are also present, the product mixture contains 1-bromo-2-chloroethane as well as 1,2-dibromoethane, even though no Cl<sub>2</sub> was ever added","The reaction mixture never changes colour under any conditions","Only 1,2-dibromoethane is ever formed, with no other products possible","Ethane is formed as the sole product of the reaction"],explain:"If Cl<sup>&minus;</sup> is present when ethene reacts with Br<sub>2</sub>, the carbocation intermediate formed in step 1 can be attacked by either Br<sup>&minus;</sup> or Cl<sup>&minus;</sup> in step 2, giving a mixture of products including 1-bromo-2-chloroethane &mdash; strong evidence for a real, freely reacting intermediate rather than a single continuous mechanism."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Why is no dichloro product ever formed in the bromination of ethene in the presence of chloride ions?",choices:["This confirms that the initial electrophilic attack on the alkene came only from Br<sub>2</sub>, since Cl<sub>2</sub> was never present to initiate the mechanism","Chloride ions never react with carbocations under any circumstances","Dichloro products are always less stable than dibromo products","The carbocation intermediate specifically repels all chloride ions"],explain:"Since Cl<sub>2</sub> itself was never added to the reaction mixture, there is no route to a dichloro product; the formation of some 1-bromo-2-chloroethane shows that Cl<sup>&minus;</sup> only becomes involved after the carbocation intermediate has already formed from attack by Br<sub>2</sub>, in the second step."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: In the acid-catalysed hydration of an alkene, what species acts as the electrophile in the first step?",choices:["H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>, via its electrophilic hydrogen","OH<sup>&minus;</sup>","The alkene's own &pi; electrons","A molecule of sulfuric acid acting as a nucleophile"],explain:"Because water alone is too weak an electrophile, a strong acid catalyst generates H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>; the electrophilic hydrogen of H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup> is attacked by the alkene's &pi; electrons, with the H&ndash;O bond breaking heterolytically to leave a carbocation and a neutral water molecule."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: In acid-catalysed hydration, why is H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup> regarded as a catalyst rather than simply a reactant?",choices:["It is consumed in the first step but regenerated in a later step when a water molecule deprotonates the intermediate, so it is not used up overall","It never actually reacts at any point in the mechanism","It is permanently converted into H<sub>2</sub> gas during the reaction","It only ever appears as a final product, never as a reactant"],explain:"H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup> is used to generate the carbocation in step 1, but a water molecule later removes a proton from the oxonium intermediate formed after nucleophilic attack by water, regenerating H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>; since it is not consumed overall, it acts as a catalyst."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Which statement correctly distinguishes an unsymmetrical alkene from a symmetrical one in the context of addition reactions?",choices:["An unsymmetrical alkene has a different number of substituents on each double-bond carbon, giving two possible carbocation intermediates on addition of an unsymmetrical reagent","A symmetrical alkene always has more carbon atoms than an unsymmetrical one","An unsymmetrical alkene can never undergo any addition reaction","Only unsymmetrical alkenes can react with halogens"],explain:"A symmetrical alkene (e.g. ethene) gives only one possible addition product, since both double-bond carbons are equivalent; an unsymmetrical alkene (e.g. propene) has non-equivalent carbons, so addition of an unsymmetrical reagent can proceed via two different carbocation intermediates."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: When propene reacts with HBr, which carbocation intermediate is more stable: the one formed if H adds to the central carbon, or the one formed if H adds to the terminal carbon?",choices:["The secondary carbocation, formed when H adds to the terminal carbon, leaving the positive charge on the central carbon","The primary carbocation, formed when H adds to the central carbon","Both carbocations are equally stable","Neither carbocation is more stable since propene cannot react with HBr"],explain:"If H adds to the terminal CH<sub>2</sub> carbon, the positive charge ends up on the central carbon, which is bonded to two alkyl groups (a secondary carbocation); this is more stable than the alternative primary carbocation (one alkyl group) formed if H instead adds to the central carbon."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: What is the major product when propene reacts with hydrogen bromide, HBr?",choices:["2-bromopropane","1-bromopropane","1,2-dibromopropane","Propane"],explain:"The reaction proceeds via the more stable secondary carbocation, which is then attacked by Br<sup>&minus;</sup>, giving 2-bromopropane as the major product; 1-bromopropane, via the less stable primary carbocation, forms only as a minor product."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: State Markovnikov's rule as it applies to the addition of a hydrogen halide to an unsymmetrical alkene.",choices:["The hydrogen atom adds to the carbon already bonded to the greater number of hydrogen atoms","The hydrogen atom always adds to the more highly substituted carbon","The halogen atom always adds to the carbon with the most hydrogen atoms","Markovnikov's rule applies only to the addition of water, never to hydrogen halides"],explain:"Markovnikov's rule states that in the addition of HX to an unsymmetrical alkene, the hydrogen adds to the carbon that already has more hydrogen atoms attached, since this routes the reaction through the more stable carbocation intermediate."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: What is the underlying reason Markovnikov's rule correctly predicts the major product of an addition reaction?",choices:["The rule reflects that the reaction proceeds preferentially through the more stable of the two possible carbocation intermediates","The rule is an arbitrary convention with no underlying chemical reason","Markovnikov's rule is based on the boiling points of the possible products","The rule only applies when the alkene is heated above 100&deg;C"],explain:"Markovnikov's rule is simply a convenient restatement of the fact that the reaction pathway through the more stable carbocation (fewer competing, less stable pathways) is strongly favoured kinetically."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: In more general terms, how can Markovnikov's rule be stated for any asymmetric electrophile adding to an asymmetric alkene?",choices:["The more electropositive part of the reacting species bonds to the less substituted (less highly substituted) carbon atom of the alkene","The more electronegative part of the reacting species always bonds to the most substituted carbon","Both parts of the reagent always bond to the same carbon atom","The rule cannot be generalized beyond the hydrogen halides"],explain:"Generalized, Markovnikov's rule states that the more electropositive part of an asymmetric reagent bonds to the less substituted carbon of the alkene, since this again routes the mechanism through the more stable carbocation intermediate."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Predict the major product when the interhalogen compound Br&ndash;Cl adds to propene, given that chlorine is more electronegative than bromine.",choices:["1-bromo-2-chloropropane, since Br<sup>&delta;+</sup> (the electrophilic end) adds to the terminal carbon, forming the more stable secondary carbocation on the central carbon","1-chloro-2-bromopropane, formed via a primary carbocation","1,2-dibromopropane, since BrCl behaves identically to Br<sub>2</sub>","Propane, since BrCl does not react with alkenes"],explain:"Because Cl is more electronegative, Br&ndash;Cl is polarized Br<sup>&delta;+</sup>&ndash;Cl<sup>&delta;&minus;</sup>. The electrophilic bromine adding to the terminal carbon gives the more stable secondary carbocation on the central carbon, which is then attacked by Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>, giving 1-bromo-2-chloropropane as the major product."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: What is meant by the 'non-Markovnikov' (anti-Markovnikov) product of an addition reaction?",choices:["The minor product that would form via the less stable carbocation intermediate, or a product deliberately favoured using specialized catalysts and conditions","The only product that can ever form from an unsymmetrical alkene","A product that violates the law of conservation of mass","A product formed only when no catalyst at all is present"],explain:"The anti-Markovnikov product is the one predicted to form via the less stable carbocation pathway; while normally only a minor product, specialized catalysts and reaction conditions have been developed that can make it the major product instead."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Why does the formation of a new covalent bond to the more stable carbocation intermediate, rather than the less stable one, dominate the product mixture?",choices:["The pathway via the more stable carbocation has a lower activation energy and proceeds faster, so it is strongly favoured kinetically over the competing, less stable pathway","Both pathways always proceed at exactly equal rates, giving a 50:50 product mixture","The less stable carbocation is actually formed in greater quantity, but reacts more slowly","Carbocation stability has no effect on which pathway is favoured"],explain:"A more stable carbocation intermediate corresponds to a lower-energy transition state leading to it, so the pathway that forms the more stable carbocation has a lower activation energy and proceeds much faster, dominating the product mixture."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Why does benzene not readily decolorize bromine water, unlike an alkene?",choices:["Benzene's delocalized ring is resonance stabilized, so addition (which would destroy this stabilization) is strongly disfavoured compared with substitution","Benzene contains no electrons capable of reacting with bromine","Benzene is actually a saturated compound, like an alkane","Bromine water reacts with benzene just as quickly as with an alkene, but with no visible colour change"],explain:"Benzene's six delocalized &pi; electrons give it an unusually high stability (resonance stabilization); an addition reaction, which would localize electrons back into alternating double bonds, is strongly disfavoured, so benzene does not react with (and does not decolorize) bromine water the way an alkene does."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Why does benzene undergo substitution rather than addition when it reacts with an electrophile?",choices:["Substitution allows the stable, delocalized aromatic ring system to be regenerated in the product, whereas addition would destroy it","Benzene is actually incapable of any chemical reaction with an electrophile","Addition reactions are always faster than substitution reactions for any organic compound","Benzene has no hydrogen atoms available to be substituted"],explain:"After an electrophile attacks the ring, loss of H<sup>+</sup> from the resulting non-aromatic intermediate regenerates the full, stabilized delocalized ring, which is a strongly favoured outcome; addition, by contrast, would leave the ring permanently disrupted, losing this stabilization."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: In the general mechanism for electrophilic substitution of benzene, what forms as the intermediate after the first step?",choices:["A non-aromatic carbocation intermediate, with the positive charge delocalized over several ring carbons","A radical intermediate with an unpaired electron on the ring","A stable, fully substituted aromatic product","An anionic intermediate with a full negative charge on the ring"],explain:"When a pair of &pi; electrons from the ring attacks the electrophile E<sup>+</sup>, a non-aromatic carbocation intermediate forms, in which the ring's delocalization is disrupted and the positive charge is spread over the remaining ring carbons."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: What happens in the second step of the electrophilic substitution of benzene?",choices:["The C&ndash;H bond on the carbon bearing both E and H breaks heterolytically, with the electron pair returning to the ring to regenerate the stable aromatic system and release H<sup>+</sup>","A second electrophile is added to the same carbon","The ring is permanently converted into a non-aromatic alkene","The entire ring structure is destroyed, releasing six separate carbon atoms"],explain:"In the fast second step, the C&ndash;H bond breaks heterolytically; the pair of electrons moves back into the ring, restoring the full, stable delocalized aromatic system, while H<sup>+</sup> is released as the ring returns to its lower-energy, substituted form."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Why is the first step of the electrophilic substitution of benzene described as having a high activation energy?",choices:["Attack on the ring temporarily disrupts the stabilizing delocalized &pi; system, which requires significant energy to overcome","No activation energy at all is needed for this step","The second step always has a higher activation energy than the first","Benzene reacts with electrophiles instantaneously, with zero activation energy"],explain:"Because forming the carbocation intermediate means temporarily losing the resonance stabilization of the aromatic ring, this first step has a relatively high activation energy and is the slow, rate-determining step of the mechanism."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: What is the electrophile involved in the nitration of benzene?",choices:["The nitronium ion, NO<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>","The nitrate ion, NO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>","Nitrous acid, HNO<sub>2</sub>","Ammonium ion, NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>"],explain:"The nitronium ion, NO<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>, generated from concentrated nitric acid and concentrated sulfuric acid, is the strong electrophile that attacks the benzene ring in nitration."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: How is the electrophile for benzene nitration generated from nitric acid and sulfuric acid?",choices:["Sulfuric acid, the stronger acid, protonates nitric acid, and the protonated species then loses a molecule of water to form NO<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>","Nitric acid protonates sulfuric acid to directly form NO<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>","The two acids react to form chlorine gas, which then generates the electrophile","NO<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup> forms spontaneously from benzene alone, with no acid mixture required"],explain:"As the stronger acid, H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> protonates HNO<sub>3</sub>; the resulting protonated nitric acid then loses a water molecule, generating the nitronium ion, NO<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>, the electrophile that attacks benzene."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: In the nitration of benzene, what happens to the H<sup>+</sup> ion released when the aromatic ring is regenerated?",choices:["It recombines with HSO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup> to reform H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>, which therefore acts as a catalyst for the overall reaction","It is permanently lost as hydrogen gas","It combines with another molecule of nitrobenzene","It is incorporated into the final nitrobenzene product"],explain:"The H<sup>+</sup> released when the carbocation intermediate loses a proton to regenerate the aromatic ring recombines with HSO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>, reforming H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>; since sulfuric acid is regenerated rather than consumed overall, it acts as a catalyst."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: What is the overall equation for the nitration of benzene to form nitrobenzene?",choices:["C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>6</sub> + HNO<sub>3</sub> &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NO<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O","C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>6</sub> + HNO<sub>3</sub> &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> + O<sub>2</sub>","C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>6</sub> + 3HNO<sub>3</sub> &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>3</sub>(NO<sub>2</sub>)<sub>3</sub> + 3H<sub>2</sub>","C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>6</sub> + NO<sub>2</sub> &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NO<sub>2</sub>"],explain:"The overall reaction, catalysed by concentrated sulfuric acid, is C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>6</sub> + HNO<sub>3</sub> &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NO<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O, substituting one ring hydrogen with a &ndash;NO<sub>2</sub> group."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Which general type of mechanism does the nitration of benzene belong to?",choices:["Electrophilic substitution","Nucleophilic substitution","Electrophilic addition","Radical substitution"],explain:"Nitration replaces a ring hydrogen with a &ndash;NO<sub>2</sub> group via attack of the electrophile NO<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup> on the delocalized ring, followed by loss of H<sup>+</sup>: an electrophilic substitution reaction."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: On an energy profile for the electrophilic substitution of benzene, how is the non-aromatic carbocation intermediate represented?",choices:["As a local minimum between two transition states, lying higher in energy than both the reactants and the final products","As the single highest point on the entire curve","As lying at exactly the same energy as the final products","As a point lower in energy than the final products"],explain:"Because the intermediate is a genuine (if short-lived and unstable) species, it appears as a local minimum between two transition states; it lies higher in energy than both the reactants and the final substituted product, since the ring's stabilizing delocalization is temporarily lost while it exists."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Which step of the benzene electrophilic substitution mechanism is rate-determining?",choices:["The first step, attack of the ring's &pi; electrons on the electrophile, since it has the higher activation energy","The second step, loss of H<sup>+</sup>, since it always has the higher activation energy","Both steps have identical activation energies","Neither step has any measurable activation energy"],explain:"The first step, in which the delocalized ring system is disrupted to form the non-aromatic carbocation intermediate, has the higher activation energy of the two steps and is therefore rate-determining."},
{ch:22,q:"Which statement correctly distinguishes a saturated compound from an unsaturated one?",choices:["A saturated compound contains only single bonds; an unsaturated compound contains at least one double or triple bond","A saturated compound always contains a halogen atom; an unsaturated one never does","An unsaturated compound can never react with any electrophile","A saturated compound always reacts faster than an unsaturated one with a given electrophile"],explain:"Saturated compounds (e.g. alkanes) contain only single bonds; unsaturated compounds (e.g. alkenes, arenes) contain one or more double or triple bonds, which is what allows them to undergo characteristic addition (or, for arenes, substitution) reactions."},
{ch:22,q:"Which type of reaction is typical of saturated compounds such as halogenoalkanes?",choices:["Substitution","Addition","Electrophilic addition","Radical addition"],explain:"Substitution reactions, in which one atom or group is replaced by another, are characteristic of saturated compounds such as halogenoalkanes, as well as of aromatic compounds; addition reactions are instead characteristic of unsaturated compounds."},
{ch:22,q:"Which type of reaction is typical of unsaturated compounds such as alkenes?",choices:["Addition","Substitution","Nucleophilic substitution","Radical substitution"],explain:"Addition reactions, in which two reactants combine to form a single product, are characteristic of unsaturated compounds containing double or triple bonds, such as alkenes."},
{ch:22,q:"According to the curly arrow convention used for reaction mechanisms, where does a curly arrow start and end?",choices:["It starts at a site of electron availability (such as a lone pair) and ends at a site of electron deficiency","It starts at a site of electron deficiency and ends at a site of electron availability","It always starts and ends on the same atom","It only ever represents the movement of a whole molecule, not electrons"],explain:"A curly arrow is drawn from the site of electron availability, such as a lone pair or a bonding pair in a breaking bond, to the site of electron deficiency, such as an atom bearing a partial or full positive charge."},
{ch:22,q:"What is the key visual difference between the curly arrow used for heterolytic fission and the arrow used for homolytic fission?",choices:["Heterolytic fission uses a full, double-barbed arrow (an electron pair); homolytic fission uses a single-barbed 'fish hook' arrow (one electron)","Both processes use exactly the same type of arrow","Heterolytic fission never uses any curly arrow at all","Homolytic fission uses a double-barbed arrow and heterolytic fission uses a fish hook"],explain:"A full, double-barbed curly arrow represents the movement of a whole electron pair (heterolytic fission, and nucleophile/electrophile reactions generally), while a single-barbed fish-hook arrow represents the movement of a single electron (homolytic fission and radical reactions)."},
{ch:22,q:"Why do halogenoalkanes act as electrophiles when reacting with a strong nucleophile, despite also acting as substrates in substitution reactions?",choices:["The carbon bonded to the halogen is electron deficient (&delta;+) and so accepts an electron pair from the attacking nucleophile","Halogenoalkanes always have a net negative charge","Halogenoalkanes can never act as electrophiles under any circumstances","The halogen atom itself accepts the nucleophile's electron pair, not the carbon"],explain:"Because the carbon bonded to the halogen is &delta;+ (electron deficient) due to the halogen's higher electronegativity, the halogenoalkane substrate itself acts as the electrophile in a nucleophilic substitution reaction, accepting the electron pair donated by the nucleophile."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Which of the following would be expected to react with hydroxide ions mainly by S<sub>N</sub>2: 1-chlorobutane or 2-chloro-2-methylpropane?",choices:["1-chlorobutane, a primary halogenoalkane with little steric hindrance","2-chloro-2-methylpropane, a tertiary halogenoalkane","Both react equally by S<sub>N</sub>2","Neither reacts by either mechanism"],explain:"1-Chlorobutane is primary, with an open, unhindered carbon bonded to the leaving group, favouring the concerted backside attack of S<sub>N</sub>2; 2-chloro-2-methylpropane is tertiary and favours S<sub>N</sub>1 instead."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Which halogenoalkane, 1-bromobutane or 1-chlorobutane, is expected to react faster with hydroxide ions, and why?",choices:["1-Bromobutane, since the weaker C&ndash;Br bond breaks more easily than the stronger C&ndash;Cl bond","1-Chlorobutane, since chlorine is a better leaving group than bromine","Both react at identical rates, since both are primary halogenoalkanes","Neither reacts with hydroxide ions under any conditions"],explain:"Because the C&ndash;Br bond enthalpy (285 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>) is lower than the C&ndash;Cl bond enthalpy (324 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>), the C&ndash;Br bond breaks more readily, making 1-bromobutane react faster regardless of which substitution mechanism operates."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Why are iodo- and bromo- compounds generally more useful than chloro- compounds as intermediates in organic synthesis?",choices:["Their weaker C&ndash;X bonds make them react more readily with a nucleophile, allowing milder reaction conditions","Their C&ndash;X bonds are stronger, making the products more stable","Iodine and bromine are cheaper than chlorine in every case","Chloro- compounds cannot undergo nucleophilic substitution at all"],explain:"Because the C&ndash;I and C&ndash;Br bonds are weaker than the C&ndash;Cl bond, iodo- and bromo- compounds react more readily with nucleophiles, often allowing a synthetic step to proceed under milder conditions."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Which best describes the overall relationship between S<sub>N</sub>1/S<sub>N</sub>2 mechanisms and the broader concept of a mechanistic model in chemistry?",choices:["S<sub>N</sub>1 and S<sub>N</sub>2 are mechanistic models that successfully explain the kinetics and stereochemistry observed experimentally for nucleophilic substitution reactions","S<sub>N</sub>1 and S<sub>N</sub>2 are directly observed, photographed events rather than inferred models","These mechanisms have no predictive power at all and are purely descriptive labels","Only one of the two mechanisms, never both, can ever be correct for any given substrate"],explain:"S<sub>N</sub>1 and S<sub>N</sub>2 are mechanistic models, inferred from kinetic data (rate equations) and stereochemical outcomes (inversion versus racemization), that successfully explain and predict the behaviour of a wide range of halogenoalkane substitution reactions."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Why might a secondary halogenoalkane give a product that is neither fully inverted nor fully racemized?",choices:["Because it reacts through a mixture of both S<sub>N</sub>1 and S<sub>N</sub>2 pathways simultaneously, giving a partial loss of optical activity","Because secondary halogenoalkanes are always completely unreactive","Because optical activity cannot be measured for any secondary halogenoalkane","Because S<sub>N</sub>1 always gives full inversion for secondary substrates"],explain:"Since secondary substrates typically react via a mixture of S<sub>N</sub>1 (giving racemization) and S<sub>N</sub>2 (giving inversion) occurring together, the measured optical activity of the product is intermediate between full retention of activity and complete loss, reflecting the relative proportion of each mechanism."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Which of the following correctly completes the comparison: S<sub>N</sub>2 is to inversion as S<sub>N</sub>1 is to ___?",choices:["Racemization","Full retention of configuration with no change at all","Homolytic fission","Electrophilic addition"],explain:"S<sub>N</sub>2 gives inversion of configuration at the reacting carbon; S<sub>N</sub>1, via its planar carbocation intermediate, gives (approximate) racemization instead."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: A chemist wants to maximize the proportion of S<sub>N</sub>2 product in a reaction of a secondary halogenoalkane. Which change would help achieve this?",choices:["Using a strong, highly concentrated nucleophile in a less polar solvent","Using a very weak nucleophile in a highly polar protic solvent","Heating the carbocation intermediate for as long as possible before adding any nucleophile","Removing the nucleophile from the reaction mixture entirely"],explain:"A strong, concentrated nucleophile increases the rate of the bimolecular S<sub>N</sub>2 pathway specifically (since it appears in the S<sub>N</sub>2 rate equation), and a less polar solvent leaves that nucleophile less solvated and more reactive, both favouring S<sub>N</sub>2 over S<sub>N</sub>1."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: A chemist wants to maximize the proportion of S<sub>N</sub>1 product in a reaction of a secondary halogenoalkane. Which change would help achieve this?",choices:["Using a weak nucleophile in a highly polar protic solvent","Using a strong, highly concentrated nucleophile in a non-polar solvent","Cooling the reaction to as low a temperature as possible with no solvent at all","Adding a large excess of a strong nucleophile"],explain:"A weak nucleophile does not compete well for direct S<sub>N</sub>2 attack, and a polar protic solvent strongly stabilizes the carbocation intermediate (and the departing anion) of the S<sub>N</sub>1 pathway, both of which favour S<sub>N</sub>1 over S<sub>N</sub>2."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Which best explains why primary carbocations are essentially never observed as stable, isolable intermediates?",choices:["With only one adjacent alkyl group to provide inductive stabilization, a primary carbocation is too unstable (high in energy) to persist, so reactions that would require one instead proceed via another pathway (e.g. S<sub>N</sub>2 instead of S<sub>N</sub>1)","Primary carbocations do not actually exist in any chemical sense","Primary carbocations are more stable than tertiary carbocations","Carbocation stability is unrelated to the number of adjacent alkyl groups"],explain:"A primary carbocation has only one neighbouring alkyl group to stabilize its positive charge through the inductive effect, making it far less stable than a secondary or tertiary carbocation; reactions of primary substrates therefore tend to avoid forming such an intermediate, proceeding instead via the concerted S<sub>N</sub>2 pathway."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: In the electrophilic addition of HBr to an alkene, which bond breaks heterolytically in the first step?",choices:["The H&ndash;Br bond, with both electrons moving onto the bromine as it leaves as Br<sup>&minus;</sup>","The C=C double bond of the alkene, breaking completely","A C&ndash;H bond elsewhere in the alkene molecule","No bond breaks in the first step"],explain:"As the alkene's &pi; electrons attack the electrophilic hydrogen of HBr, the H&ndash;Br bond breaks heterolytically, sending both bonding electrons onto bromine and releasing it as Br<sup>&minus;</sup>, while a carbocation forms on the alkene carbon that did not bond to H."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: What is the final step that completes the addition of HBr to an alkene, after the carbocation intermediate has formed?",choices:["The bromide ion, Br<sup>&minus;</sup>, released in step 1 attacks the carbocation, forming the new C&ndash;Br bond","The carbocation reacts with another molecule of HBr","The carbocation spontaneously loses a proton to reform the original alkene","The bromide ion is permanently expelled from the reaction mixture"],explain:"In the fast second step of HBr addition, the bromide ion generated in step 1 acts as the nucleophile, attacking the carbocation and forming the halogenoalkane product."},
{ch:22,q:"Which statement correctly distinguishes addition reactions from substitution reactions?",choices:["Addition combines two reactants into a single product; substitution replaces one atom or group in a compound with a different one","Addition always produces two separate products; substitution always produces only one","Substitution is characteristic of alkenes; addition is characteristic of halogenoalkanes","Addition and substitution are two names for exactly the same process"],explain:"Addition reactions combine two separate reactants into a single product (characteristic of unsaturated compounds); substitution reactions replace one atom or group in a compound with a different one (characteristic of saturated and aromatic compounds)."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Which factor, among steric hindrance, carbocation stability, nucleophile strength and solvent polarity, would NOT normally be expected to change the preferred mechanism for a fixed, given substrate if only the reaction conditions (not the substrate) are varied?",choices:["None of these factors is fixed by the substrate alone; nucleophile strength and solvent polarity can both be varied independently of the substrate to shift the mechanism","Carbocation stability, since it depends only on the solvent used","Steric hindrance, since it depends only on the nucleophile used","All four factors are entirely unrelated to reaction conditions"],explain:"While steric hindrance and (largely) carbocation stability are set by the substrate's own structure, nucleophile strength/concentration and solvent polarity are reaction conditions that can be varied independently, shifting the balance between S<sub>N</sub>1 and S<sub>N</sub>2 for the same substrate."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Why does the S<sub>N</sub>1 mechanism not depend on the concentration of the nucleophile in its rate equation?",choices:["The nucleophile is only involved in the fast second step, which occurs after (and does not affect the rate of) the slow, rate-determining first step","The nucleophile is actually involved in both steps equally","S<sub>N</sub>1 reactions do not involve a nucleophile at all, at any point","Nucleophile concentration is always zero in an S<sub>N</sub>1 reaction"],explain:"Because the rate-determining step of S<sub>N</sub>1 is the unimolecular breaking apart of the substrate (forming the carbocation), which happens before the nucleophile becomes involved in the fast second step, changing the nucleophile's concentration has no effect on the overall observed rate."},
{ch:22,q:"HL: Which of the following is the best summary of why Markovnikov's rule and the S<sub>N</sub>1/S<sub>N</sub>2 framework are both described as carbocation-based models?",choices:["Both explain their respective major products or mechanisms in terms of the relative stability of carbocation intermediates (tertiary &gt; secondary &gt; primary)","Neither model has any connection to carbocations at all","Markovnikov's rule applies only to substitution, while S<sub>N</sub>1/S<sub>N</sub>2 applies only to addition","Carbocation stability is relevant to S<sub>N</sub>1 but completely irrelevant to Markovnikov's rule"],explain:"Both frameworks rest on the same underlying principle: a more stable carbocation intermediate (favoured by more alkyl substituents) is formed preferentially, whether this determines the major product of an unsymmetrical addition (Markovnikov's rule) or the favoured mechanism of a substitution (S<sub>N</sub>1 for substrates giving stable carbocations)."}
]
